Hi,

Thanks for the reply.

I just saw ur soln. What I did was read the dataframe as it is into
hdfs.
Then I used a map like this:
map = expression({
for(i in 1:length(map.values[[grep("shape",map.keys)]])){
rhcollect(map.values[[grep("shape",map.keys)]][[i]],list(len =
map.values[[grep("Length",map.keys)]][[i]], breadth=
map.values[[grep("Breadth",map.keys)]][[i]] , height =
map.values[[grep("height",map.keys)]][[i]] ))

It worked quite well.
I now want to ask u abt the last part in ur reply, i.e. regarding
working with huge datasets. (sorry for bringing this up in the same
thread. Will create a new one if u want).

I currently have to work on a flat file of 1GB, but very soon the data
size will be scaling to the order of 100s GBs.(and need to run
mapreduce on that). But unfortunately I am struggling with the 1GB
itself. First I am facing problems with reading the data (1GB single
'.dat' file). Obviously things like read.csv fail as read.csv creates
objects in the R environment (my RAM  is also only 2GB per machine).
So I used a combination of filehash and sqldf packages and wrote the
data to a database(on the disk) and that too I could select only the
required columns(which are less for the time being) from the .dat
file.
Use statement like this to read data into database:
dumpDF(sqldf("select x,y,z from f", dbname=tempfile()), dbName="dbx")
f is a connection to the .dat file I am reading data from.
which takes around 3 mins for creating dbx that is 500mb.
Then I write this (smaller) data (from database) into hdfs, which I
can subsequently use for mapreduce.

My concern is when data is gonna scale I cannot be using these kind of
statements as they are not only time consuming, but I doubt they would
even successfully work. (If I do select * from f, then it gets Killed
and R quits)

So what I thought I should ideally try is copy the huge data into
hdfs(master) (not through R) which would get split across the data
nodes automatically. And now I should run my mapreduce with the input
(ifolder) being the location of the split files. But would this be
correct as I dont think map can take the data in this format as input?
(If I say inout[1] as text what would my map.keys and map.values look
like?)

And next I want to ask if R(and Rhipe) is suitable for doing large
data analytics on hadoop? Would the mapreduce jobs written using Rhipe
run successfully for 100s of GBs of data(and in a shorter span of time
compared to doing this using other techniques). Or is using Java or
Pig etc. be better?

Sorry for digressing from the topic of this post and Thanks in
advance.

